libfabric: NIXL_LIBFABRIC_EFA_UNSOLICITED_WRITE_RECV keeps EFA unsolicited write receive on

Built against the EFA installer's libfabric headers, which the patched plugin needs, the plugin turns EFA's unsolicited
write receive off on every endpoint, and nothing turns it back on. With it off, every incoming write with immediate
data uses up a posted receive. A decode rank that takes many KV writes at once runs out of them, its endpoint fails
("Internal queue pair error") and the whole decode server stops. With the variable set to 1 the plugin keeps the
feature on, as the PyPI 1.5.0 wheels do (their headers lack the option). Unset, the plugin behaves as upstream.

Upstream turns the feature off because, with it on, remote write completions can be lost under load, so that
notifications never arrive (ai-dynamo/nixl#2494). SGLang's KV transfers do not depend on them: they carry no
notification, and the prefill learns each transfer's outcome from its own handles and tells the decode in its
KV_STATUS message over ZMQ. Both ends of a connection need the same setting; the EFA provider does not refuse a pair
that disagrees but falls back to slower emulated writes between them.

Upstream: not proposed yet (from v1.5.2, upstream disables the feature whatever the headers, #2494)
Tests: none in this patch

diff --git a/src/utils/libfabric/libfabric_rail.cpp b/src/utils/libfabric/libfabric_rail.cpp
index 491d16f4..b66e32d3 100644
--- a/src/utils/libfabric/libfabric_rail.cpp
+++ b/src/utils/libfabric/libfabric_rail.cpp
@@ -588,8 +588,14 @@ nixlLibfabricRail::nixlLibfabricRail(const std::string &device,
 
         if (provider_name == "efa") {
 #ifdef HAVE_FI_OPT_EFA_USE_UNSOLICITED_WRITE_RECV
-            // Disable unsolicited write recv for EFA RDM to reduce CQ overflow likelihood
-            const bool use_unsolicited_write_recv = false; // Set to false to disable the feature
+            // Disabled by default to reduce CQ overflow likelihood: every incoming write-with-imm then consumes a
+            // posted receive instead of completing straight into the CQ. A target that takes many small writes from
+            // many peers at once (a disaggregated-inference decode rank receiving KV from 64 prefill ranks) runs its
+            // posted receives dry, and with infinite RNR retry (below) the senders' retries flood its NIC.
+            // NIXL_LIBFABRIC_EFA_UNSOLICITED_WRITE_RECV=1 keeps the feature on for such deployments.
+            const char *uwr_env = getenv("NIXL_LIBFABRIC_EFA_UNSOLICITED_WRITE_RECV");
+            const bool use_unsolicited_write_recv =
+                uwr_env != nullptr && (std::strcmp(uwr_env, "1") == 0 || std::strcmp(uwr_env, "true") == 0);
             ret = fi_setopt(&endpoint->fid,
                             FI_OPT_ENDPOINT,
                             FI_OPT_EFA_USE_UNSOLICITED_WRITE_RECV,
@@ -599,7 +605,8 @@ nixlLibfabricRail::nixlLibfabricRail(const std::string &device,
                 NIXL_WARN << "fi_setopt FI_OPT_EFA_USE_UNSOLICITED_WRITE_RECV failed for rail "
                           << rail_id << ": " << fi_strerror(-ret) << " - continuing anyway";
             } else if (ret == 0) {
-                NIXL_INFO << "Successfully disabled unsolicited write recv for rail " << rail_id;
+                NIXL_INFO << "Unsolicited write recv " << (use_unsolicited_write_recv ? "enabled" : "disabled")
+                          << " for rail " << rail_id;
             } else if (ret == -FI_ENOSYS || ret == -FI_ENOPROTOOPT) {
                 NIXL_DEBUG << "FI_OPT_EFA_USE_UNSOLICITED_WRITE_RECV not supported for rail "
                            << rail_id << " (provider: " << provider_name << ") - skipping";
